Consider a cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) wireless network consisting of L access points (APs), each equipped with N antennas and connected through a fronthaul to one or more central processing units (CPUs), jointly serving K single-antenna users on the same time–frequency resources. Let h_{l,k} ∈ ℂ^N denote the (block-fading) channel between AP l and user k, and let p^{ul}_k ≥ 0 and η_{l,k} ≥ 0 denote the uplink transmit power of user k and the downlink power coefficient used by AP l for user k, subject to per-user uplink power constraints p^{ul}_k ≤ P^{ul}_{max} and per-AP downlink power constraints ∑_k η_{l,k} E{‖w_{l,k}‖²} ≤ P^{dl}_{max}, where w_{l,k} are precoding/combining vectors. Define an implementation of uplink and downlink power control as scalable if the computational complexity per AP and per user, the amount of CSI sharing across the fronthaul, and the signaling overhead remain bounded as L, K → ∞.

Develop a framework of uplink and downlink power-control schemes for CF-MMIMO that are scalable in the above sense and that simultaneously address:
(i) sum spectral efficiency maximization;
(ii) user fairness, e.g. max–min or proportional-fairness criteria on the per-user achievable rates log_2(1 + SINR_k);
(iii) mitigation of the near-far effect arising from heterogeneous large-scale fading;
(iv) inter-user interference control;
(v) the trade-off between fully centralized optimization at a CPU and fully distributed computation at individual APs/users;
(vi) operation under only partial CSI sharing between APs and CPUs;
(vii) the design and provable performance limits of machine-learning and reinforcement-learning-based power-control policies in this setting.

Specifically, determine to what extent any scalable power-control rule (heuristic and fully distributed, or sub-optimal and centralized with only partial CSI sharing) can approach the performance of the corresponding network-wide optimal but unscalable power allocation, and characterize the fundamental trade-offs among the criteria (i)–(vii).

